# S4 legacy OAuth mapping: ruling needed

The old schema records a controller/client→Agent binding but cannot prove
whether the operation that first created it completed. This is a source
question before adoption, not observed loss or a deployment change. The
current Worker configuration keeps hosted MCP closed.

The exact7177 schemas are:

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS control (op TEXT PRIMARY KEY, fingerprint TEXT NOT NULL, action TEXT NOT NULL, agent TEXT, state TEXT NOT NULL, answer TEXT, not_after TEXT NOT NULL, created TEXT NOT NULL)
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS agents (agent TEXT PRIMARY KEY, handle TEXT NOT NULL, key TEXT, state TEXT NOT NULL, created TEXT NOT NULL)
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS oauth_agent (controller TEXT NOT NULL, client_id TEXT NOT NULL, agent TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE, handle TEXT NOT NULL, created TEXT NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (controller, client_id))
```

The mapping has no originating op, pending flag or fingerprint. Control rows
have an op/fingerprint/target/state, but do not store client_id/controller or
the full signed body. Native Agent rows exposed by Registry give only
agent/handle/key/state/created. A completed control answer generally names
agent/grant/codeShown/state_digest, not its originating mapping client.
The fingerprint binds a supplied request; it cannot identify which old mapping
that request INSERTed versus reused. Same target or created timestamp is not
ownership: repeated requests can share a clock value and reuse the same row.
The source admits multiple reserved grant operations against an already
mapped Agent. Neither a sole convenient candidate nor NULL proves ownership.

## Minimal new-row ownership

Add nullable `pending_op TEXT` transactionally, default NULL on old rows.
Use three disjoint meanings in that one column:

- NULL: legacy origin unresolved. Do not claim, delete, credit or silently
  call it established.
- A nonempty valid control op: this exact new operation provisionally owns
  the new mapping. Control op and fingerprint/target plus every mapping field
  must still match before settlement.
- Empty string: a new mapping was explicitly confirmed after the exact
  successful handoff. Empty is not a valid control op.

New op bytes obey the existing1–128 UTF-8-byte bound. Charge those bytes once
with the existing five mapping values+ROW_OVERHEAD128 and control reservation
CONTROL_ROW_BYTES4096, under the existing control budget16MiB with1MiB kept
for retire. Clearing owner to empty credits only its op bytes, once. Removing
a definitively refused, exact owned pending mapping credits its full logical
mapping bytes once. Established empty-marker rows are never claimed by a new
operation; they survive another grant's refusal.503/temporary denial/known
committed-but-withheld work retains the pending owner and exact reservation.

The ALTER adds no logical value bytes to legacy rows. NULL/empty both add
zero UTF-8 value bytes; the existing row-overhead convention remains. No
migration refund, speculative charge, tombstone deletion or capacity expansion
follows. All migration/settlement changes remain inside their owning SQL
transactions; mismatch retains/refuses rather than guessing an owner.

## Continuity choice for legacy NULL

A conservative unconditional branch retains the row and refuses new
mapping-changing consent with existing503 temporarily_unavailable. It never
deletes uncertain rows. This protects pending exclusivity but blocks new
consent on affected old bindings, even if some were established. Existing
valid access/refresh admission does not read this Registry mapping, so this
choice does not itself revoke all old tokens. Ordinary expiry, fresh role,
Agent retirement and reuse rules still apply. No code proves the affected
legacy population here.

A narrower fresh-reauthorization alternative may avoid that consent loss
without pretending to recover the old origin:

1. Obtain a NEW explicit signed authorization for the SAME stored controller,
   client and Agent. Verify trusted current client context, exact public Agent
   identity and fresh same-head Native key/controller/subset/actor authority.
   Do not infer fresh consent from the old row alone.
2. In one Registry transaction, compare the entire unchanged legacy mapping
   and verify there is NO other unresolved reserved grant control for that
   Agent, across all clients. Reserve the fresh op in that same transaction.
   All legacy/new grant entry paths must honor this exclusive control fence;
   an expired notAfter alone does not prove an old handoff never committed.
3. Retain the legacy NULL mapping untouched while the fresh handoff runs.
   Record new-op exclusivity in its existing control reservation, not by
   pretending the old row was created by it. Repeated same-op work resumes.
4. Only after an actual successful matching Agent grant, final fresh authority
   and exact control/candidate/mapping comparisons, mark the row confirmed
   empty. This establishes the NEW authorization now; it proves no historical
   completion. On definitive refusal retain the old NULL row unchanged and
   settle only the fresh operation. Unavailable/uncertain work stays reserved.

This alternative adds one explicit fresh consent where needed and requires a
reviewed all-path reservation fence. It may refuse when any legacy pending
handoff remains or complete owner facts are unavailable. Existing code does
not yet prove that fence; no implementation or unconditional recovery claim
is made. It creates no secret/progress file, counting mechanism or new recovery
task. Planner can choose it, retain/refuse, or specify another bounded policy
before adoption. The note assumes no permanent deferral or automatic cleanup
of unknown rows. Existing authorized retirement/list/revoke semantics remain
separate and cannot be silently weakened to make migration convenient.

Unchanged new-mapping/grantExisting work can proceed with the explicit pending
owner and exact fresh reads. Its legacy branch stays conditional on this
ruling. Full creation fresh admission, issuer/Agent final checks, witness
assembly and actual c+d/main rebind remain required. No migration, compiler,
parser/helper, import, test, control, build, deployment or other body ran.
